    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe specification required rejecting a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with a 400 response. I added the validation in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`, at the point where the supplied registration date is read (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java:122`):\n\n```java\nLocalDate effectiveRegistrationDate = owner.getRegistrationDate();\nif (effectiveRegistrationDate == null) {\n    effectiveRegistrationDate = LocalDate.now();\n} else if (effectiveRegistrationDate.isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {\n    throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST,\n        \"registrationDate must not be later than the current server date\");\n}\n```\n\nDesign notes:\n- The check applies only to a **supplied** date (a null date still defaults to today), matching \"a supplied registrationDate.\"\n- It runs on the raw supplied value **before** `rollToBusinessDay`, so a future date is rejected rather than silently rolled.\n- It uses `ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, \u2026)`, consistent with the existing validation idioms in this controller (telephone, email, postcode, address).\n\nThe build passes: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 the new `errorRejectsFutureDate` acceptance test (posting `registrationDate: 2999-01-01`) now returns 400 as expected.",
